  "description": "<p>camel toolbox only ships with support for XSLT 1.0 which is ancient by modern standards. Requests have come in for more modern support for 2 and 3. </p>\n\n\n<p>The Solr indexer applies the transform via Camel's plain XSLT component — <tt>SolrRouter.java:170</tt> does {{toD(\"xslt:$</p>\n{header.CamelIndexingTransformation}\n<p>\")}}, and the project depends on <tt>camel-xslt</tt> (<a href=\"https://claude.ai/epitaxy/local_8d990485-d089-4753-b36e-65afd03eaadd\" class=\"external-link\" rel=\"nofollow noreferrer\">pom.xml:161</a>).</p>\n\n<p>That component delegates to the JDK's built-in JAXP <tt>TransformerFactory</tt> (the Xalan-derived XSLTC processor bundled with the JVM), which implements only XSLT 1.0 / XPath 1.0. Saxon is nowhere on the classpath, so there's no XSLT 2.0/3.0 support.</p>\n\n<p>If you wanted 2.0/3.0 support, the standard route would be swapping in <tt>camel-xslt-saxon</tt> and using <tt>xslt-saxon:</tt> URIs — Saxon-HE would give you XSLT 3.0. That's worth keeping in mind for the Camel 4 upgrade too, since the component split (<tt>camel-xslt</tt> vs <tt>camel-xslt-saxon</tt>) carries over there unchanged.</p>",
